    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "we checked the elements with a thermal camera and the heat is totally even across the plate. moisture isn't an issue either since we run the fabric through the pre-dryer right before. \n\nit's just weird. it's like certain tiny bits of the ink just vanish during the press cycle. almost like something is interfering with the gas phase of the sublimation in those specific spots. is there any way for something to just... prevent the ink from bonding in small, random patches even if temps and pressure are fine?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If the particular parts of the print are missing, and you've ruled out heat and moisture as factors, another direction to investigate is the composition of the polyester fabric itself. Check if there are non-reactive sections within the fabric blend that only material of the ink can react with sufficiently. Also, consider the possibility of static electricity interacting with the ink during the transfer process, especially if the polyester blend is particularly prone to static. Balancing the surface tension between the ink and the polyester material might resolve the issue. If static is indeed the culprit, adding antistatic agents before printing could help achieve even bonding, provided that such agents are compatible with your printing process.",
      "source": "target",
